        "surveyType": "Targeted Survey",
        "surveySubType": "Targeted Survey",
        "narrative": "<p>Temperature and dissolved oxygen (DO) measurements were collected from the deepest basin in Lake Two on August 1st, 2025, to evaluate the quantity and quality of cold, oxygenated water (i.e., oxythermal habitat) available to Lake Whitefish, the most sensitive coldwater species present in this lake (Cisco, also known as 'Tullibee', are also present). During the summer, Lake Whitefish require a layer of oxythermal habitat that contains water temperatures below 17.2 degrees C (63.0 degrees F) and dissolved oxygen concentrations above 3.0 mg/L. Based on the profile from the deepest basin (maximum sampled depth 32.8 feet), the water temperature decreased below 17.2 degrees C (63.0 degrees F) at 21.1 feet and the dissolved oxygen concentration decreased to 3.0 mg/L at 19.8 feet at the time of sampling. Therefore, there was not a layer of water with sufficient temperature and dissolved oxygen for Lake Whitefish. <br> \n <br> \nFurther, the temperature at which dissolved oxygen decreased to 3.0 mg/L (i.e., TDO3, a single variable to characterize oxythermal habitat) was 18.6 degrees C (65.5 degrees F). TDO3 values warmer than 17.2 degrees C (63.0 degrees F) indicate that oxythermal conditions were unsuitable for Lake Whitefish at the time of the survey.  <br> \n <br> \nData collected by the Minnesota Department of Natural Resources during the late summer period of maximum oxythermal stress (July 26th to Aug 24th) in 2024  indicates suitable oxythermal habitat, with 8.7 feet of suitable habitat available. When adequate oxythermal habitat is unavailable, Lake Whitefish are squeezed into unfavorable, warmer water conditions and experience higher levels of stress. <br> \n</p>",
        "surveyID": "-321",
        "surveyDate": "2025-08-01",
        "lengths": {},
        "fishCatchSummaries": []
      },
      {
        "narrative": "<p>Lake Two is in Ecological Lake Class 7, which consists of 41 lakes in northeast Minnesota that are large, have very irregular shoreline shapes, and have very soft and infertile water.  Lake Two is smaller than average for this lake class, and its shoreline drops off into deep water more quickly than usual.Lake Two stratifies thermally in midsummer and has poor oxygen (less than 2 ppm) below about 25 ft.  The Kawishiwi River flows through Lake Two with a large flow.  Fish may move freely through the inlet (from Lake Three and Lake Four), but rapids at the two outlets (both to Lake One) prevent upstream fish movement.  The lake bottom near shore is mostly boulder and ledgerock, with small amounts of rubble, gravel, and muck.  Aquatic plants are sparse.The total catch of fish in 1996 (all species combined) was lower than normal for this lake class, but was similar to catches in previous surveys in this lake in 1963 and 1978.  Lake Class 7 is one of the most biologically productive lake classes in northeast Minnesota.  Lake Two has less productive shallow area along the shoreline, and may have less chemically productive water.  Also, flowage-type lakes like Lake Two often do not produce as many fish as lakes without major rivers flowing through them.Fish populations in 1996 were dominated by northern pike.  Pike numbers in 1996 were higher than normal for this lake class and were also higher than in previous surveys on this lake.  Pike sizes averaged 22 in (2.4 lb) which is normal for this lake class and similar to previous surveys on this lake.  The largest pike in 1996 was 32 in (7.7 lb).  Pike reproduction was very even from year-to-year and pike growth (22 in at age four) was normal for the area.Walleye numbers in 1996 were lower than normal for this lake class and were also lower than in previous surveys.  The lower walleye catch in 1996 may be due to the early date of the assessment (mid June) when compared to previous surveys which were done in mid July and early August.  Walleye sizes in 1996 averaged 13 in (0.8 lb) which is normal for this lake class and identical to previous surveys on this lake.  Walleye reproduction has been even from year-to-year.  Walleye growth (12 in at age four) is slower than normal for the area.Cisco numbers in 1996 were normal for this lake class and similar to previous catches in this lake.  Cisco sizes were small, averaging 9 in; the largest was 13 in.  White sucker numbers were normal for this lake class, but sucker sizes were small.  Other fish species, captured in low numbers in 1996, were bluegill sunfish, burbot (eelpout), rock bass, and yellow perch.  Lake whitefish, present in low numbers in previous surveys, were absent in 1996. </p>",
